<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="nitrogen remobilization from senescing leaves" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6549" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; In plants, the protein levels in seeds influence seed survival in natural settings and help determine the seeds&apos; nutritional value and processing properties when they are grown for human and animal consumption. A large fraction of this protein content may be derived from the remobilization of nitrogen from proteins in senescing leaves |CITS: [11244107][16690166][17244043]|. This nitrogen can be transported in the form of amino acids, prinicipally glutamine, through the phloem. Improving the efficiency of this process could lead to the production of seed-based crops with higher protein levels |CITS: [20202167]|. One of the enzymes in this pathway, pyruvate, orthodiphosphate dikinase (PPDK), plays a prominent role in photosynthesis in C4 plants such as |FRAME: TAX-4577 &quot;&lt;i&gt;Zea mays&lt;/i&gt; (maize)&quot;|, yet it is also expressed in C3 plants, such as |FRAME: ORG-5993|. This pathway may help to explain the physiological function of PPDK in C3 plants |CITS: [20202167]|. Interestingly, in all plant species studied to date, both a cytosolic and a chloroplastic isoform of the PPDK enzyme are generated from alternative promoters upstream of the same chromosomal locus. Over-expression of the cytosolic form of this enzyme in Arabidopsis during senescence can lead to an increase in seed weight and seed nitrogen content indicating that this form of the enzyme may be important for nitrogen remobilization |CITS: [20202167]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Portions of this pathway likely occurs in several subcellular compartments, notably, the cytosol, mitochondria, and chloroplast. The pyruvate that initally feeds into this pathway may be derived from the deamination of some amino acids, such as threonine, glycine, serine, alanine, and cysteine. Starting with pyruvate, after the sequential activities of cytosolic PPDK and PEP carboxylase, the |FRAME: OXALACETIC_ACID| (OAA) produced can flow into the |FRAME: PWY-5690 TCA cycle| in the mitochondria where it can be converted to citrate. The citrate may be metabolized in the mitochondria or the cytosol by aconitase. The subsequent transformation of |FRAME: THREO-DS-ISO-CITRATE isocitrate| into |FRAME: 2-KETOGLUTARATE| can be catalyzed by |FRAME: CPLXQT-51| in the mitochondria |CITS: [16527867]| or by |FRAME: AT1G65930-MONOMER| in the cytosol |CITS: [20199623][20202167]|. In the chloroplast, this |FRAME: 2-KETOGLUTARATE| carbon skeleton can then be transformed into a nitrogen-transporting amino acid by the activities of glutamate synthase and glutamine synthase. The resulting |FRAME: GLN| can then be exported to seeds through the phloem |CITS: [20202167]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE" />
    <Author name="dreher" />
  </Authors>
