    <DataNode elementId="AT2G05710-MONOMER" textLabel="Aconitate hydratase 3, mitochondrial" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="AT2G05710-MONOMER" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="695" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">ACO3 is an aconitase that can catalyze the conversion of |FRAME: CIT| to |FRAME: THREO-DS-ISO-CITRATE| through a |FRAME: CIS-ACONITATE| intermediate, based on the phenotype of reduced aconitase activity in an &lt;i&gt;aco3&lt;/i&gt; mutant |CITS: [17013749]|. ACO3 may participate in the TCA cycle and other primary metabolic pathways and it is believed to accumulate in the mitochondria and the cytosol |CITS: [17013749]|. &lt;i&gt;aco3&lt;/i&gt; mutants have an elevated level of transcripts for |FRAME: AT2G28190|, which encodes a superoxide dismutase, and show enhanced tolerance to oxidative stress. Interestingly, one member of the aconitase family, |FRAME: AT4G35830-MONOMER ACO1|, was shown to specifically bind to the 5&apos; UTR of |FRAME: AT2G28190| &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [17013749]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT2G05710-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="Aconitase 3" />
      <Property key="Synonym_2" value="mACO1" />
      <Property key="Synonym_3" value="Citrate hydro-lyase 3" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-33229, RXN-10603, RXN-ARA-720" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="3" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT2G05710.1" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT2G05710" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_17013749" />
    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT1G65930-MONOMER" textLabel="NADP+-dependent isocitrate dehydrogenase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="AT1G65930-MONOMER" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="450" centerY="695" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The cICDH protein acts as an NADP+-dependent isocitrate dehydrogenase. It is believed to act in the cytosol |CITS: [20199623]|. Unlike the mitochondrial NAD+-dependent isocitrate dehydrogenases (see |FRAME: ISOCITRATE-DEHYDROGENASE-NAD+-RXN EC 1.1.1.41| ), this enzyme is not expected to participate in the Krebs / TCA cycle directly, however, the observation of reduced levels of succinate, fumarate, and malate in &lt;i&gt;icdh&lt;/i&gt; mutant plants suggests that it is involved in the metabolic flux toward the TCA cycle. However, it might play a more important role in maintaining redox homeostasis and contributing to pathogen defense responses |CITS: [20199623]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G65930-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-4512" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT1G65930.1" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT1G65930" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_20199623" />
    </DataNode>
